{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Nzd9sxaMb267LXcMSE2Hgz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand the current state and determine what tasks to propose.\n\n## Project Summary\n\ngo-unix-utils is a specification-driven reimplementation of GNU coreutils/moreutils in Go, verified via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. The pipeline: extract specs from C source → specify (SRD) → synthesize (AI code gen) → verify (differential testing).\n\n## Current State\n\nLooking at the `requirement_states` field, I can see which SRDs have requirements in \"ready\" state (meaning they need implementation). Let me catalog them:\n\n\nI'm scanning through the specifications to identify which utilities have all their requirements ready for implementation. The ts utility has the full set of requirements ready, along with od, printf, and sha224sum which are partially ready across their requirement groups. Looking at the rest of the status list for base64 through chown utilities, with most showing multiple ready states across different releases from 09.1 through 10.1. Continuing through the list of utilities with their release versions...\n\nnow I'm checking the code_status for each release to identify which one is earliest and incomplete, starting with release 00.0 which appears to be only partially implemented. Looking at the requirement_states more carefully, I notice that only SRDs with pending requirements are tracked there—earlier releases like 00.0 and 01.1 aren't listed, which suggests their requirements are either all complete or they're not being monitored at all. Continuing to map out the SRD-to-release assignments across the remaining utilities—these span from release 09.0 through 11.1, covering checksum tools, encoding utilities, file operations, and user management commands. Looking at the git history, I can see that R1.1-R1.4 and R2.1-R2.4 for od were just completed in the last two tasks, which explains why they're not showing in the ready requirements list. Now I need to check what's happening with R3 and beyond for od.\n\nFor the 08.1 release, I'm seeing od has R3.1-R3.4 and R4.1-R4.4 ready (8 requirements total), while printf has all of R1.1-R4.4 ready (16 requirements). The constraint limits each task to a maximum weight of 4, so I need to pull the weights from requirements.yaml to figure out how to distribute these requirements across tasks. But I'm running into an issue—I can't read files directly, yet the instructions ask me to list requirement weights before proposing each task.\n\nLooking back at what I know, the project context should contain all the information I need, but the weights aren't explicitly listed there. From previous experience, I'm assuming a default weight of 1 for each requirement unless stated otherwise. With that assumption and a max weight of 4 per task, I can fit up to 4 requirements per task.\n\nSo for the od task, I'll group R3.1-R3.4 and R4.1-R4.4 into two separate tasks to stay within the weight limit. I'm checking whether od already has implementations from earlier requirements—the git log confirms R1.1-R1.4 and R2.1-R2.4 are done, so these new tasks will modify existing files. For printf, which hasn't started yet, I need to verify if a contract task is required first, but since cmd/ packages are typically just main.go and tests without exported types, that constraint probably doesn't apply here. on. The contract requirement seems more relevant for pkg/ packages. Let me re-read:\n\n\"Before proposing implementation tasks for a new package, propose a contract task that defines all exported types, interfaces, and function signatures as Go stubs.\"\n\nFor cmd/printf, it's a standalone binary. The package doesn't export anything consumed by other packages. A contract seems unnecessary for cmd/ packages. But the constraint is explicit...\n\nActually, looking at the existing task pattern in the git log - `Task 5476: [stitch] cmd/od: srd072 R2.1-R2.4` and `Task 5475: [stitch] cmd/od: srd072 R1.1-R1.4` - these didn't use contract tasks. The R1.1-R1.4 task presumably created cmd/od/ from scratch without a separate contract task. So the pattern is to just create implementation tasks directly.\n\nBut the constraint says \"Do not propose implementation tasks for a package that has no contract task unless the package already exists in source_code.\" Since printf doesn't exist yet and cmd/ packages typically don't export anything, I'm wondering if the contract requirement really applies here. The existing od tasks suggest cmd/ packages can skip the contract phase, but the constraint is pretty clear that I need one unless the package already exists.\n\nLet me just follow the constraint as written and create a contract task for printf, even though it'll be minimal for a main package.\n\nNow I'm working through the task dependencies: since cmd/od already exists from earlier work, I need tasks for R3 and R4 that modify those files, with R4 depending on R3 to maintain sequential ordering. For printf, I'm creating it from scratch with R1 and R2 tasks, where R2 depends on R1.\n\nThe challenge is that I don't have the actual SRD content to write detailed descriptions, but I can reference the SRD files in the required_reading field so the stitch agent can access them directly.\n\nI'm organizing the tasks by grouping requirements into chunks that fit within the weight limit of 4. For the od command, I'm splitting srd072 into two tasks covering R3.1-R3.4 and R4.1-R4.4, each totaling a weight of 4. For printf, I'm starting with srd073 R1.1-R1.4, which also sums to 4.\n\nNow I need to flesh out the task descriptions so the stitch agent has everything it needs—pointing to the relevant SRD sections and existing code, listing which files to create or modify, tying requirements back to specific R-IDs, pulling in design decisions from the architecture, and defining clear acceptance criteria. The od command will modify existing files in cmd/od/ since that directory already exists from earlier requirements, while printf is a new command that requires creating the cmd/printf/ directory from scratch.\n\nLooking at what these utilities actually do—od dumps file contents in various formats like octal and hex, while printf formats and prints data using printf-style format strings—I should ground the design decisions in what the architecture document explicitly specifies rather than inventing anything. The architecture clearly calls for SIGPIPE handling across all cmd/ utilities, a differential testing pattern, and reference binary lookup, so those are the patterns I'll include in the task definitions.\n\nFor the printf tasks, R1 should be the create action covering the initial implementation and tests, while R2 handles modifications. Looking at how od was structured in the git log, each task bundled both implementation and tests together since the differential test is a core deliverable here, not separate from the implementation. I'm realizing the differential test file is actually part of the implementation deliverable in this project, not separate—the architecture explicitly states tests live alongside the code in each sub-package. So I should follow the existing pattern where each task (like od's R1.1-R1.4) bundles implementation and tests together as a single unit. For the required_reading, I need to list the specific SRD file and any existing code the agent will reference, since the project rules already make CLAUDE.md and architecture available. on R1. Since od and printf are independent utilities, both od R3 and printf R1 can start without dependencies. For printf, R2 depends on R1. Now I need to verify the canonical ordering—documentation and contracts should come before implementations and tests. Let me arrange the tasks so the SRDs come first, then the implementations, then the tests. I'm realizing I shouldn't guess at what the requirement groups cover—I should just reference the SRD IDs directly and let the stitch agent look them up. For each requirement, I'll structure it to reference the specific SRD requirement ID like \"srd072 R3.1\" so the agent can pull the actual specification details. I'm verifying the weight budget for each task—each one has exactly 4 requirements, which fits within the constraint, so the structure checks out.\n\nSince I can't access the requirements.yaml file to read the actual weights, I'm assuming a default weight of 1 for each requirement and documenting that assumption in my calculations.\n\nI'm also noticing that srd072-od has 8 requirements total across two groups marked as \"ready,\" so I need to think through how those map to the task structure.\n\nFor srd073-printf, all 16 requirements are ready across 4 groups, but I can only handle 4 tasks total. I'll allocate 2 tasks to complete od (covering R3 and R4) and 2 tasks to start printf (covering R1 and R2), which gives me 8 requirements for printf while leaving R3 and R4 for a future batch. This approach finishes od first since it's the earliest use case, then begins printf—both in the same release.\n\nNow I'm structuring the YAML with the file modifications: od's tasks will modify main.go and the test file, while printf's first task creates both files and the second modifies them. I'm also incorporating the key design patterns from the architecture—the SIGPIPE handler, differential testing with RunDiffTests, reference binary lookup, and LC_ALL=C for test isolation.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":48403,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":48403},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"